(module crux-shape-barrier-vacuous)
;; THE CLOSED-BARRIER CONDITION, NOT ENFORCED. driver-spec.txt section 6:229-231
;; requires every dispositioned-out row to cite a barrier from the closed list,
;; and rfc_to_implementation.py:493 raises it through `require_spec` rather than
;; `require`, which is what makes it the one spec-defined halt in stage G and
;; therefore `stopped` rather than `failed` (proposal section 3.5's rule). This
;; body answers `true` whatever the counts, so an exclusion citing a barrier
;; outside the list passes and the run continues to gate J.
;;
;; WHAT IT COSTS IN THE REFERENCE, which is why the condition is enforced twice.
;; Stage G rejects the disposition output and gate J re-checks a resumed run's
;; inventory (scripts/tests/test_rfc_pipeline_integration.py:325-329, and the
;; redundancy is called deliberate there). A vacuous stage-G check does not make
;; the run unsafe on its own, because J still holds; it makes the STAGE-LEVEL
;; halt disappear, so a run stops at J with no record of which stage produced
;; the offending row, on a pipeline whose whole output is an audit trail.
;;
;; DISCRIMINATION, measured at v0.14.87 over this body:
;;
;;   [G6-CLOSED]    -> REFUTED
;;   [G6-NOVACUOUS] -> REFUTED
;;
;; BOTH refute, and that is expected rather than a defect in the mutant.
;; [G6-CLOSED] is an equality between two booleans, so it determines `result`
;; entirely and every other post about this function is a consequence of it.
;; [G6-NOVACUOUS] is stated separately in shape.llmll for the reason
;; [V7-NO-FLOOR] is stated separately in validate.llmll: a reader looking for
;; "an unlisted barrier must fail" should find it by name rather than derive it.
;; gate.llmll sets the same precedent with [S6-CORE] and [S6-REPORTED]. No body
;; can break one of a determining post and its consequence while keeping the
;; other, so this crux is not the discriminating kind and does not claim to be.
(def barrier-condition-met? [excluded-count: int listed-count: int] -> bool

  (pre (and (>= excluded-count 0)
            (and (>= listed-count 0)
                 (<= listed-count excluded-count))))

  (post (= result (= excluded-count listed-count)))

  (post (=> (< listed-count excluded-count) (not result)))

  true)
